<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! lares.lararium.status ~ the bare door: how the house stands, what it pins, which keys it routes

The bare `lares` reads `lares mu=00 aperture=beat`, this carrier's terminal spelling. It answers in three parts and moves nothing, ever: where an act waits it names the door that takes it.

The status line stands the same for every caller: the revision the checkout stands at against develop's head (N behind) · lit or dark, with the standing the vessel published · lit by · faces · canon lag and the pending removals · keep due · a begun half waiting. A field the door cannot read in one cheap step names the door that reads it, never a guess. A dark house answers as a report ("the house stands dark"), never a refusal.

The pins layer as TiddlyWiki stacks layers, so the asker's context picks: the house list (canon, lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/lararium/pins), then the worn face's own (its daemon wiki, lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/lararium/pins), then a mandate's (the mandate record's `pins` field; a spirit's summons names its mandate). A person sees house and face; a summoned spirit sees house, face and mandate. Every pin rides as a runnable call sigil, one per line: a person reads it, a spirit copies and runs it as written. Any hand re-pins by writing the list record in its own layer.

The keys line names the act keys the keypad routes (`keys: get= put= delete= rename= filter= act= rite= bag= …`); the full door list moved behind `lares help`.

The two dials read as the `set` panel reads them (lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot #/set-and-setting): `mu` the lens, its cells below naming each view and the door it runs until the carriers of the next landings fold them into one call; `aperture` the range, defaulting to `beat`. The terminal's own door walks the views; these rows stand as its testimony.

